      { t: "Step 6 — EGO = EYAN = AEYN = NINE", d: "Letter-shuffling: EGO rearranged to EYAN to AEYN, then declared to equal NINE ('something from nothing'). Honest grade: this is wordplay, not linguistics (the teaching's frame). Anagram doesn't equal etymology — you can rearrange any letters into anything. The 'something from nothing' gloss is the teaching's metaphysics riding on a Scrabble move. Fun? Yes. Evidence? No." },
      { t: "Step 7 — EL-N-EVE = ELEVEN, and 911", d: "Same class: EL + N + EVE sounds like 'eleven' in English — a phonetic coincidence, not a revelation (the teaching's frame). 911: 9+1+1 = 11, and the chart crowns it 'the name of God.' Note the move: the emergency number's charge (crisis! call now!) is being borrowed for mystique. The arithmetic is correct; the coronation is poetry. And 'eleven' as a 'master number' is numerology's frame, not mathematics'." },
      { t: "Step 8 — SUN = SON = NOSE", d: "The sun/son pun works ONLY in English — they're homophones in one language. In Hebrew (shemesh/ben), Greek (helios/huios), Latin (sol/filius), the words are unrelated (say it). A pun that dies in translation was never an eternal truth — it was a local joke. 'Nose' (breath?) is the chart free-associating. The honest rule: if the revelation requires English, it's English literature, not cosmic law." },
      { t: "Step 9 — The hexagram math: correct arithmetic, mystical freight", d: "The chart's numbers: a triangle's angles are 60° each; 60 × 3 = 180 — the true angle sum of a Euclidean triangle (Documented geometry). 1+8+0 = 9 — correct digit-summing. The math is RIGHT; what's loaded is the meaning: that 9 is therefore divine. Digit-sums are real arithmetic; '9 = completion/divinity' is numerology (the teaching's frame). Separate the calculation from the coronation." },
      { t: "Step 10 — Compass, square, and the prism", d: "The Masonic square's 90° right angle: real (that's what squares are for). The '911' overlay on the compass-and-square: the teaching's frame. The prism splitting white light into the rainbow at the poster's top: real optics — Newton, 1666 (Documented). The chart is at its best here: genuine phenomena (light splits; squares square) with the teaching's poetry laid over them. Learn to see the two layers separately and you'll never be fooled by the blend again." },
      { t: "Step 11 — Why word-magic is seductive", d: "The honest psychology: humans are pattern engines — we see faces in clouds and messages in coincidences (apophenia; Established). English puns FEEL like revelations because the 'click' of recognition mimics the 'click' of understanding. Word-magic hijacks that click. Knowing this doesn't kill the fun — it just moves it from the 'hidden truth' shelf to the 'poetry' shelf, where it belongs." },
      { t: "Step 12 — The practice: test, don't just taste", d: "The chart's own method, turned on itself: take any three equations from any word-magic poster and run the dictionary test — (1) What language is each word from? (2) What do scholars say the root means? (3) Does the equation survive translation? You'll find the same ratio every time: a little gold (El, Lucifer), a lot of glitter. The practice IS the teaching: remember, don't merely believe." },
      { t: "Step 13 — What to keep and what to drop", d: "Keep: El (real), Lucifer = light-bringer (real Latin), the correct arithmetic, the prism, and the master rule — remember, don't just believe. Drop: ATOM = ADAM, LIGHT = EVE, EGO = NINE, EL-N-EVE = ELEVEN as linguistics; the sun/son pun as cosmic law; 911 as anyone's name. The chart is a poem that thinks it's a proof. Read it as the poem." }
